WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.330 --> 00:00:08.463
- Thanks, Priya, and thanks for having me.

00:00:09.896 --> 00:00:12.396
I'm really honored to be here.

00:00:13.850 --> 00:00:16.050
So I'm a philosopher of science,

00:00:16.050 --> 00:00:19.200
interested in causation
and causal modeling.

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:21.430
I work on both sort of
fairly concrete issues

00:00:21.430 --> 00:00:23.943
about the nuts and bolts
of causal modeling,

00:00:24.780 --> 00:00:27.030
but also on sort of much more abstract

00:00:27.030 --> 00:00:28.980
and high level issues

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:32.550
about the foundations of causal inference,

00:00:32.550 --> 00:00:35.640
and how causation and causal inference

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:39.210
sort of fits in a physics
based picture of the world.

00:00:39.210 --> 00:00:42.330
And so today, my talk's gonna
be much more in the abstract,

00:00:42.330 --> 00:00:43.163
high level side.

00:00:43.163 --> 00:00:45.300
It's gonna be fairly disconnected

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:47.640
from the day-to-day methodological issues

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:50.670
that you might face in science.

00:00:50.670 --> 00:00:52.920
But essentially what I'm gonna focus in on

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:56.700
is this phenomenon that
has puzzled physicists

00:00:56.700 --> 00:00:59.550
and philosophers of
science for a long time,

00:00:59.550 --> 00:01:01.173
which is that in some sense,

00:01:02.356 --> 00:01:05.520
it's easier to infer the
past than the future.

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:07.290
I'm going to try to tell a story

00:01:07.290 --> 00:01:09.300
as to why that's the case,

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:11.670
that I think brings out
some interesting facts

00:01:11.670 --> 00:01:16.670
about causal inference and
how the physics of our world

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:20.160
puts constraints on the
kinds of causal inferences

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:24.843
we can make about the past or the future.

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:28.360
So, what do I mean when I say

00:01:29.670 --> 00:01:32.340
that it's easier to infer
the past than the future?

00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:33.210
I'll tell you in a minute,

00:01:33.210 --> 00:01:35.793
if I can find a way to move my slides.

00:01:37.050 --> 00:01:37.883
All right.

00:01:38.850 --> 00:01:39.900
So, what do I mean when I say

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:41.730
it's easier to infer the
past than the future?

00:01:41.730 --> 00:01:44.700
Well, I can give you a couple
of perfectly mundane examples

00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:47.430
to get to see what I mean.

00:01:47.430 --> 00:01:49.275
You know, if you want to figure out

00:01:49.275 --> 00:01:51.810
what the weather was like exactly
10 years ago in New Haven,

00:01:51.810 --> 00:01:53.730
you can of course consult
the weather records,

00:01:53.730 --> 00:01:55.440
but it's impossible to predict

00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:56.490
what the weather is gonna be like

00:01:56.490 --> 00:01:58.590
in exactly 10 years from now.

00:01:58.590 --> 00:02:00.690
If you see the footprints in your garden,

00:02:00.690 --> 00:02:04.140
you can figure out, you know,
a fox visited last night,

00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:06.180
but there's no sign in your garden

00:02:06.180 --> 00:02:09.240
that tells you a fox gonna
visit tomorrow night.

00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:10.770
You know, you can see the dent in the car

00:02:10.770 --> 00:02:12.900
and figure out there's
been a collision recently.

00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:16.860
But again, no mark on
the car that tells you

00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:17.910
the car's gonna be involved

00:02:17.910 --> 00:02:19.650
in a collision in the future, right?

00:02:19.650 --> 00:02:21.483
So, perfectly mundane examples.

00:02:22.350 --> 00:02:25.203
Looking at more interesting
scientific examples,

00:02:26.250 --> 00:02:28.500
we have all those
sciences of the deep past,

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:32.910
like cosmology and geology
and evolutionary biology

00:02:32.910 --> 00:02:36.120
that give us very detailed
and reliable accounts

00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:37.950
of the sometimes very distant past,

00:02:37.950 --> 00:02:39.734
but we don't have anything

00:02:39.734 --> 00:02:41.250
like a comprehensive scientific account

00:02:41.250 --> 00:02:43.380
of the distant future, right?

00:02:43.380 --> 00:02:44.850
And so that's the sort of phenomenon

00:02:44.850 --> 00:02:46.980
I'm interested in trying to explain,

00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:51.980
at a very general level.

00:02:52.650 --> 00:02:53.880
And you might think, you know,

00:02:53.880 --> 00:02:57.150
the explanation for this sort
of asymmetry of inference,

00:02:57.150 --> 00:02:58.233
it's obvious.

00:02:59.250 --> 00:03:01.020
It has to do with the
fact that the future,

00:03:01.020 --> 00:03:03.210
by definition, hasn't happened yet, right?

00:03:03.210 --> 00:03:07.080
So it's still open, and that's
why we can't know about it.

00:03:07.080 --> 00:03:09.543
But the past is settled and it's fixed,

00:03:10.650 --> 00:03:13.533
and that's why we can
know about it, right?

00:03:14.370 --> 00:03:15.870
So there's a couple of issues with that,

00:03:15.870 --> 00:03:18.030
but perhaps the most obvious one

00:03:18.030 --> 00:03:20.580
is that we do, of course,

00:03:20.580 --> 00:03:24.630
make plenty of successful
predictions about the future.

00:03:24.630 --> 00:03:28.353
Again, using perfectly mundane
examples to make the point:

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:31.260
we can predict when the next solar eclipse

00:03:31.260 --> 00:03:33.693
is going to be in the US.

00:03:34.710 --> 00:03:37.860
I can see the smoke rising
from my cooking pan,

00:03:37.860 --> 00:03:40.560
and figure out that the
fire alarm is going to beep

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:44.670
in a few seconds, so on and so on, right?

00:03:44.670 --> 00:03:47.700
Again, all this is perfectly obvious.

00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:50.010
So, the contrast here seems to be really

00:03:50.010 --> 00:03:53.250
that when it comes to making
successful predictions,

00:03:53.250 --> 00:03:55.830
we're sort of restricted
to very special context,

00:03:55.830 --> 00:03:58.800
like context having to
do with isolated systems,

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:00.690
like in a solar system example,

00:04:00.690 --> 00:04:03.120
or with the immediate future,

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:05.340
like in my cooking pan example.

00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:07.860
Or we can perhaps make predictions

00:04:07.860 --> 00:04:09.240
about the longer term future,

00:04:09.240 --> 00:04:12.390
but they have to be sort of
very broad brush, very vague.

00:04:12.390 --> 00:04:13.770
Like I might predict that, you know,

00:04:13.770 --> 00:04:16.470
the Russian invasion of
Ukraine is gonna have

00:04:16.470 --> 00:04:19.620
drastic consequences on
the European geopolitics

00:04:19.620 --> 00:04:21.690
in 10 years, but might not be able

00:04:21.690 --> 00:04:25.920
to make any more specific
predictions than this.

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:28.530
But when we turn to
inferences toward the past,

00:04:28.530 --> 00:04:30.090
there's no such restriction, right?

00:04:30.090 --> 00:04:34.050
Again, we can make very
detailed, very precise inferences

00:04:34.050 --> 00:04:37.110
about sometimes the very distant past,

00:04:37.110 --> 00:04:40.530
and about systems like
the weather or species,

00:04:40.530 --> 00:04:45.210
that are not at all isolated
from their environment

00:04:45.210 --> 00:04:48.990
and whose dynamics may be in
fact quite chaotic, right?

00:04:48.990 --> 00:04:53.250
And the point is blunt
distinction between an open future

00:04:53.250 --> 00:04:55.171
and a fixed past is not going to be able

00:04:55.171 --> 00:04:58.920
to explain that contrast, right?

00:04:58.920 --> 00:05:02.610
So presumably, instead, the answer

00:05:02.610 --> 00:05:06.060
for why it's easier to infer
the past than the future

00:05:06.060 --> 00:05:09.120
has something to do with the
physics of our world, right?

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:13.053
It has to have some sort
of physical explanation.

00:05:14.370 --> 00:05:16.050
But that's somewhat of a mystery here,

00:05:16.050 --> 00:05:19.950
because the answer or the explanation,

00:05:19.950 --> 00:05:23.670
it's not going to come from the
fundamental laws of physics,

00:05:23.670 --> 00:05:25.590
because the fundamental laws of physics

00:05:25.590 --> 00:05:27.540
are all time symmetric.

00:05:27.540 --> 00:05:29.010
They don't really distinguish

00:05:29.010 --> 00:05:30.873
between the past and the future.

00:05:32.790 --> 00:05:34.410
You know, we've known since Newton

00:05:34.410 --> 00:05:38.370
that there's no real intrinsic
direction of determination

00:05:38.370 --> 00:05:40.620
in the laws of physics.

00:05:40.620 --> 00:05:43.800
Earlier states of the world
determine later states

00:05:43.800 --> 00:05:47.730
just as much as later states
determine earlier states.

00:05:47.730 --> 00:05:49.983
So, in relativity theory, for example,

00:05:51.922 --> 00:05:53.100
you know what's gonna happen at time zero,

00:05:53.100 --> 00:05:55.470
here it's gonna be fixed
by the cross-section

00:05:55.470 --> 00:05:57.540
in the backward light cone,

00:05:57.540 --> 00:05:58.770
but it's also going to be fixed

00:05:58.770 --> 00:06:01.170
by the cross-section in
the future light, right?

00:06:02.460 --> 00:06:05.133
So there's no asymmetry here.

00:06:07.560 --> 00:06:10.140
And so, that means,

00:06:10.140 --> 00:06:12.360
you know, from the perspective
of the laws of physics,

00:06:12.360 --> 00:06:14.340
there's no real reason
to expect the future

00:06:14.340 --> 00:06:17.163
to be harder to predict than the past.

00:06:18.870 --> 00:06:20.040
So, there's this mystery

00:06:20.040 --> 00:06:24.174
that has puzzled physicists
and philosophers of science

00:06:24.174 --> 00:06:26.580
for a while.

00:06:26.580 --> 00:06:28.200
Some of the people in this room

00:06:28.200 --> 00:06:30.180
have worked on this as well.

00:06:30.180 --> 00:06:33.150
Here is some of the relevant literature.

00:06:33.150 --> 00:06:35.010
The starting point of all of this

00:06:35.010 --> 00:06:39.690
was Hans Reichenbar's work
in The Direction of Time.

00:06:39.690 --> 00:06:41.550
Now, one idea you find in Reichenbach

00:06:41.550 --> 00:06:46.550
is that this asymmetry has
to do with entropy increase,

00:06:47.220 --> 00:06:48.990
and with the second law of thermodynamics.

00:06:48.990 --> 00:06:51.420
So, there's a very sort
of influential tradition

00:06:51.420 --> 00:06:54.630
of exploring that line of thought.

00:06:54.630 --> 00:06:56.640
There's also an idea
you find in Reichenbach

00:06:56.640 --> 00:06:59.160
that the asymmetry of inference

00:06:59.160 --> 00:07:01.110
has something to do with causation,

00:07:01.110 --> 00:07:04.140
and with the distinction
between causes and effects,

00:07:04.140 --> 00:07:06.060
and with what Reichenbach called

00:07:06.060 --> 00:07:08.490
the principle of the common cause.

00:07:08.490 --> 00:07:09.780
So that's more the line of thought

00:07:09.780 --> 00:07:11.580
I'm interested in exploring today,

00:07:11.580 --> 00:07:15.540
and what I'm going to
say sort of systematizes

00:07:15.540 --> 00:07:18.210
some of the things that
Reichenbach says in his book,

00:07:18.210 --> 00:07:22.830
but that I think people haven't
really picked up on too much

00:07:22.830 --> 00:07:26.130
in this literature.

00:07:26.130 --> 00:07:29.043
And so, the big picture
is something like this.

00:07:30.930 --> 00:07:34.150
The kinds of inferences
we make toward the past

00:07:35.549 --> 00:07:36.870
and toward the future,

00:07:36.870 --> 00:07:40.170
they are and they have
to be causal inferences.

00:07:40.170 --> 00:07:43.770
They have to involve cause
and effect reasoning, right?

00:07:43.770 --> 00:07:45.870
So inferences toward the past,

00:07:45.870 --> 00:07:50.580
they almost always involve
deriving the past cause

00:07:50.580 --> 00:07:52.680
from a present effect, right?

00:07:52.680 --> 00:07:55.773
Only a fox could have
caused those footprints.

00:07:56.820 --> 00:07:59.670
You know, only an organism
with this and that feature

00:07:59.670 --> 00:08:02.250
could have produced a fossil
that's in front of me,

00:08:02.250 --> 00:08:03.123
and so on.

00:08:04.320 --> 00:08:06.210
And inferences we make toward the future,

00:08:06.210 --> 00:08:11.210
they involve deriving a future
effect from a present cause.

00:08:12.990 --> 00:08:13.980
The smoke rising,

00:08:13.980 --> 00:08:16.563
that's going to cause
the fire alarm to beep.

00:08:17.730 --> 00:08:19.650
The Russian invasion,
that's going to cause

00:08:19.650 --> 00:08:22.983
this and that consequence 10
years down the line, and so on.

00:08:25.743 --> 00:08:27.210
It's no surprise, of course,

00:08:27.210 --> 00:08:30.713
that all the inferences we make

00:08:30.713 --> 00:08:32.310
with the past or the future are causal,

00:08:32.310 --> 00:08:37.080
because, you know, to infer
an event from another one,

00:08:37.080 --> 00:08:39.900
there needs to be some kind
of correlation between them.

00:08:39.900 --> 00:08:42.570
And correlation is usually
at least going to require

00:08:42.570 --> 00:08:46.680
some sort of causal connection
between those two events.

00:08:46.680 --> 00:08:49.140
And so, the hypothesis here

00:08:49.140 --> 00:08:52.830
is that maybe there's something

00:08:52.830 --> 00:08:54.540
about the cause effect distinction

00:08:54.540 --> 00:08:59.540
that makes it easier to
infer the causes of events

00:09:01.260 --> 00:09:03.060
than their effects.

00:09:03.060 --> 00:09:05.640
Maybe there's something about
the cause effect distinction

00:09:05.640 --> 00:09:09.180
that makes it easier to
infer causes from effects,

00:09:09.180 --> 00:09:12.303
than to infer effects from causes.

00:09:13.410 --> 00:09:16.380
And I apologize for the
mismatch in the slide.

00:09:16.380 --> 00:09:18.573
This wasn't like that on my computer.

00:09:21.000 --> 00:09:22.920
Of course, to make good
on that hypothesis,

00:09:22.920 --> 00:09:24.660
you're going to need to explain

00:09:24.660 --> 00:09:27.240
what exactly distinguishes
causes from effects.

00:09:27.240 --> 00:09:29.760
And you know, why that distinction
would sort of give rise

00:09:29.760 --> 00:09:31.083
to this asymmetry.

00:09:32.130 --> 00:09:33.090
And that question,

00:09:33.090 --> 00:09:35.057
the distinction between
causes and effects,

00:09:35.057 --> 00:09:37.680
that's also something that
has puzzled physicists

00:09:37.680 --> 00:09:39.960
and philosophers of
science for a long time.

00:09:39.960 --> 00:09:42.060
Again, because the
fundamental laws of physics,

00:09:42.060 --> 00:09:43.360
they're not really causal.

00:09:44.451 --> 00:09:45.420
They don't really distinguish
between past and future.

00:09:45.420 --> 00:09:48.000
They don't have a
direction of determination.

00:09:48.000 --> 00:09:49.080
So that's sort of a mystery

00:09:49.080 --> 00:09:52.950
as to how something like a
robust cause effect distinction

00:09:52.950 --> 00:09:56.340
emerges from physics.

00:09:56.340 --> 00:09:58.950
And like many people, I think,

00:09:58.950 --> 00:10:00.660
the answer at the end of the day

00:10:00.660 --> 00:10:05.370
comes from the work done in
the causal model in tradition,

00:10:05.370 --> 00:10:09.303
which itself systematizes
some ideas of Reichenbach.

00:10:10.860 --> 00:10:12.063
And the thought here,

00:10:13.237 --> 00:10:14.760
and I assume this is
familiar to everybody,

00:10:14.760 --> 00:10:18.420
is that the kinds of
causal structure we see

00:10:18.420 --> 00:10:20.520
in our environment,

00:10:20.520 --> 00:10:24.780
they can be represented in
the form of asical graphs

00:10:24.780 --> 00:10:27.221
that represent relationships
of direct causation

00:10:27.221 --> 00:10:29.250
between variables.

00:10:29.250 --> 00:10:31.020
And the sort of key principle,

00:10:31.020 --> 00:10:34.770
or the key assumption is
that there are going to be

00:10:34.770 --> 00:10:37.230
some principles that relate

00:10:37.230 --> 00:10:39.370
to the causal structure of those systems

00:10:40.250 --> 00:10:42.120
to the statistical structure.

00:10:42.120 --> 00:10:45.513
And the most important one is
the causal mark of condition,

00:10:46.530 --> 00:10:49.470
that tells you that exogenous variables,

00:10:49.470 --> 00:10:54.150
sort of initial causal conditions,

00:10:54.150 --> 00:10:56.340
the causal inputs to your system,

00:10:56.340 --> 00:10:59.100
they're not gonna be
correlated with one another.

00:10:59.100 --> 00:11:02.073
The values are going to
be fixed independently.

00:11:03.990 --> 00:11:05.883
Sorry, in the model over here,

00:11:08.010 --> 00:11:11.013
S is not gonna be correlated with W.

00:11:13.950 --> 00:11:17.610
But by contrast, endogenous variables,

00:11:17.610 --> 00:11:19.200
the effects of the system,

00:11:19.200 --> 00:11:23.013
they can and usually are
correlated with each other,

00:11:24.000 --> 00:11:26.310
at least when they have
a common cause, right?

00:11:26.310 --> 00:11:28.860
Common causes are going
to create correlations

00:11:28.860 --> 00:11:30.300
between effects.

00:11:30.300 --> 00:11:32.490
So X and Y, for example, here,

00:11:32.490 --> 00:11:36.000
are going to be correlated
because they have a common cause.

00:11:36.000 --> 00:11:38.610
So, at a very general level,

00:11:38.610 --> 00:11:41.220
that's what the cause
effect distinction is about.

00:11:41.220 --> 00:11:44.670
It's about causes are the variables

00:11:44.670 --> 00:11:47.400
that tend to be statistically
independent of one another,

00:11:47.400 --> 00:11:50.273
and therefore are sort of the
causal inputs of the system.

00:11:50.273 --> 00:11:52.350
And the effects are those variables

00:11:52.350 --> 00:11:56.553
that are correlated with one another.

00:11:58.140 --> 00:12:00.990
And what I want to try to
suggest in the rest of the talk

00:12:00.990 --> 00:12:05.730
is that it is that
asymmetry, the causal mark,

00:12:05.730 --> 00:12:09.300
or the asymmetry encoded in
the causal mark of condition

00:12:09.300 --> 00:12:12.780
that explains why it's easier
to infer causes from effects

00:12:12.780 --> 00:12:14.310
than vice versa.

00:12:14.310 --> 00:12:16.890
And the claim a bit
more precisely is this:

00:12:16.890 --> 00:12:19.290
the causal mark of
condition is going to entail

00:12:19.290 --> 00:12:21.450
in a number of circumstances

00:12:21.450 --> 00:12:24.090
that the probability of an effect,

00:12:24.090 --> 00:12:28.293
given a single one of its
causes, is going to be very low,

00:12:29.490 --> 00:12:31.320
except if you're in a special context

00:12:31.320 --> 00:12:33.750
where, you know, you're dealing
with an isolated system,

00:12:33.750 --> 00:12:36.450
or again, the effect is in the
immediate future of the cause

00:12:36.450 --> 00:12:37.653
or something like that.

00:12:39.510 --> 00:12:42.460
But by contrast, because
the causal mark of condition

00:12:43.890 --> 00:12:46.920
doesn't require effects to be
independent of one another,

00:12:46.920 --> 00:12:48.963
it allows for correlations between them.

00:12:49.920 --> 00:12:52.410
It's going to entail that
the probability of a cause

00:12:52.410 --> 00:12:54.870
given its effect can be very high,

00:12:54.870 --> 00:12:58.890
even if the cause is in the
very, very distant past.

00:12:58.890 --> 00:13:00.840
And even if the system we're dealing with

00:13:00.840 --> 00:13:03.453
is not isolated at all.

00:13:04.530 --> 00:13:06.600
I'll give you an example
to try to sort of make this

00:13:06.600 --> 00:13:09.333
a bit more concrete in a second.

00:13:10.800 --> 00:13:14.310
But one other idea I wanna
put on the table here

00:13:14.310 --> 00:13:18.660
is that when you think
about causal inferences

00:13:18.660 --> 00:13:21.333
and inferences to the
future or to the past,

00:13:22.885 --> 00:13:24.170
there's a kind of mystery

00:13:24.170 --> 00:13:29.170
of how limited beings like
us can ever infer anything

00:13:29.790 --> 00:13:31.500
about the past or the future,

00:13:31.500 --> 00:13:34.320
because the physics of
our world seems to put

00:13:34.320 --> 00:13:36.273
a lot of obstacles to that.

00:13:41.003 --> 00:13:41.836
The problem is that the
dynamical laws of physics

00:13:43.800 --> 00:13:46.770
that govern our world, they're global,

00:13:46.770 --> 00:13:50.880
in the sense that they relate
global states of the universe.

00:13:50.880 --> 00:13:52.590
So again, in relativity theory,

00:13:52.590 --> 00:13:56.130
to figure out what happens
at a space and point,

00:13:56.130 --> 00:13:59.220
you need information about
the entire cross-section

00:13:59.220 --> 00:14:00.060
of the light cone.

00:14:00.060 --> 00:14:01.890
Is it backward or forward?

00:14:01.890 --> 00:14:03.450
And, you know, typically
that's going to be

00:14:03.450 --> 00:14:06.570
an enormous spatial region, right?

00:14:06.570 --> 00:14:09.330
And of course we can't
observe global states, right?

00:14:09.330 --> 00:14:10.163
We have to make do

00:14:10.163 --> 00:14:14.250
with very localized and
partial observations.

00:14:14.250 --> 00:14:16.050
So, it's kind of a
mystery of how we can use

00:14:16.050 --> 00:14:18.630
those local observations to ever predict

00:14:18.630 --> 00:14:23.430
or retrodict what happens
at other times, right?

00:14:23.430 --> 00:14:28.380
Even this sort of drastic
under-determination.

00:14:28.380 --> 00:14:30.180
And the thought here is going to be

00:14:30.180 --> 00:14:32.880
that the causal mark of condition,

00:14:32.880 --> 00:14:34.833
or the causal structure of our world,

00:14:35.760 --> 00:14:37.440
it removes that obstacle.

00:14:37.440 --> 00:14:42.440
It makes it possible to make
inferences from local events,

00:14:44.100 --> 00:14:46.950
but it's going to remove that obstacle

00:14:46.950 --> 00:14:49.443
more towards the past
than towards the future,

00:14:50.520 --> 00:14:51.420
as I might say.

00:14:51.420 --> 00:14:53.510
There's still gonna be,

00:14:53.510 --> 00:14:55.920
you know, an enormous
epistemic predicament

00:14:55.920 --> 00:14:58.803
in trying to figure out
what the future is like.

00:15:02.070 --> 00:15:04.923
And a remark for the
philosophers in the room:

00:15:05.760 --> 00:15:07.410
I mentioned as this was a line of thought

00:15:07.410 --> 00:15:09.540
about, you know, the
asymmetry of inference,

00:15:09.540 --> 00:15:12.930
that this has to do with
entropy increase and so on.

00:15:12.930 --> 00:15:15.450
The best version we
have of this story today

00:15:15.450 --> 00:15:17.340
is due to David Albert.

00:15:17.340 --> 00:15:21.540
And you might be wondering,
"What's the relationship here?"

00:15:21.540 --> 00:15:23.340
Maybe I'll say a few words
about this at the end

00:15:23.340 --> 00:15:24.858
if I have the time.

00:15:24.858 --> 00:15:27.090
But very roughly the idea
is that the two stories

00:15:27.090 --> 00:15:29.070
are sort of compatible and complementary,

00:15:29.070 --> 00:15:31.410
but there are also some important nuances

00:15:31.410 --> 00:15:34.920
about, you know, how central
the causal asymmetry is

00:15:34.920 --> 00:15:37.350
in the picture of the world,

00:15:37.350 --> 00:15:41.043
and how temporal asymmetry
emerged from physics.

00:15:46.770 --> 00:15:49.710
I'm just going to work
through an example here

00:15:49.710 --> 00:15:52.560
to sort of try to make good
on those various claims

00:15:52.560 --> 00:15:53.613
I've made here.

00:15:54.960 --> 00:15:57.030
It's not gonna be a
very interesting example

00:15:57.030 --> 00:15:59.940
of, you know, historical
scientific inference.

00:15:59.940 --> 00:16:00.930
You know, I'm a philosopher.

00:16:00.930 --> 00:16:05.910
I use mundane examples because
they help make the points

00:16:05.910 --> 00:16:06.903
more simply.

00:16:08.910 --> 00:16:10.815
But the idea is, you know,

00:16:10.815 --> 00:16:11.970
this is an example we can use to think

00:16:11.970 --> 00:16:15.930
about inferences at
other times in general,

00:16:15.930 --> 00:16:18.360
including the sort of very
sophisticated inferences

00:16:18.360 --> 00:16:22.713
you find in evolutionary
biology and geology, and so on.

00:16:23.983 --> 00:16:26.280
So what you have here on
the left is The Mayflower

00:16:26.280 --> 00:16:29.913
departing from Plymouth in
England to America in 1620.

00:16:31.440 --> 00:16:33.510
And here on the right is a random plaque

00:16:33.510 --> 00:16:37.440
I found on the internet
in Harwich in the UK,

00:16:37.440 --> 00:16:41.580
that commemorates the departure
of The Mayflower in 1620.

00:16:41.580 --> 00:16:42.690
It says a number of things,

00:16:42.690 --> 00:16:47.340
but one thing it says is that
this here happened, right?

00:16:47.340 --> 00:16:49.650
And obviously there's
a causal relationship

00:16:49.650 --> 00:16:50.643
between those two.

00:16:53.101 --> 00:16:54.300
So, you know, even if you know nothing

00:16:54.300 --> 00:16:56.433
about The Mayflower or anything,

00:16:57.900 --> 00:16:59.280
as long as you read English, you know,

00:16:59.280 --> 00:17:01.710
you can walk by that plaque, read it,

00:17:01.710 --> 00:17:05.130
and then come to learn that
this event happened, right?

00:17:05.130 --> 00:17:06.963
If you go around reading plaques,

00:17:08.100 --> 00:17:10.140
you'll get it right most of the time.

00:17:10.140 --> 00:17:11.643
Plaques are really reliable.

00:17:13.380 --> 00:17:16.440
So it's very, very easy to
make that inference here

00:17:16.440 --> 00:17:17.550
from the effect to the cause.

00:17:17.550 --> 00:17:18.383
But of course, you know,

00:17:18.383 --> 00:17:20.460
nobody who had witnessed this here

00:17:20.460 --> 00:17:23.610
would've been able to predict
the existence of the plaque

00:17:23.610 --> 00:17:25.010
four centuries later, right?

00:17:25.950 --> 00:17:28.953
If you had predicted that
you would've been insane.

00:17:30.840 --> 00:17:32.160
So, that's a case where, you know,

00:17:32.160 --> 00:17:33.930
if you want to put it
in terms of probability,

00:17:33.930 --> 00:17:35.640
is the probability of the
effect given the cause

00:17:35.640 --> 00:17:36.473
is very high.

00:17:38.636 --> 00:17:41.490
Sorry, the probability of
the effect given the cause

00:17:41.490 --> 00:17:42.970
is very low, but the
probability of the cause

00:17:42.970 --> 00:17:45.270
given the effect is very high.

00:17:45.270 --> 00:17:46.980
So again, I want to think about it.

00:17:46.980 --> 00:17:50.160
What explains this at a
very general abstract level?

00:17:50.160 --> 00:17:52.293
What explains this asymmetry here?

00:17:54.690 --> 00:17:59.373
When you looking first at the
cause to effect direction,

00:18:01.230 --> 00:18:03.570
it's obvious why nobody would've predicted

00:18:03.570 --> 00:18:05.610
the existence of the plaque
four centuries later,

00:18:05.610 --> 00:18:07.710
if they had witnessed the
departure of the ship, right?

00:18:07.710 --> 00:18:11.010
Because the departure of the ship

00:18:11.010 --> 00:18:12.960
obviously was not causally sufficient

00:18:12.960 --> 00:18:14.400
for the existence of the plaque.

00:18:14.400 --> 00:18:16.890
'Cause staggeringly large
number of other factors

00:18:16.890 --> 00:18:18.630
had to be in place in 1620

00:18:18.630 --> 00:18:20.910
to later lead to that event, right?

00:18:20.910 --> 00:18:22.440
So that includes, you know,

00:18:22.440 --> 00:18:25.290
all the factors that would
later make The Mayflower

00:18:25.290 --> 00:18:28.410
or its departure a significant
event in world history.

00:18:28.410 --> 00:18:30.750
So, you know, facts about the weather

00:18:30.750 --> 00:18:32.730
that would ensure that
the ship doesn't sink

00:18:32.730 --> 00:18:34.440
and gets forgotten, you know,

00:18:34.440 --> 00:18:37.800
facts about the Wampanoag people

00:18:37.800 --> 00:18:39.030
who would provide assistance

00:18:39.030 --> 00:18:42.027
to the ship's passengers
in Massachusetts and so on.

00:18:42.027 --> 00:18:45.690
And also facts that would later lead

00:18:45.690 --> 00:18:50.280
to the emergence of plaque
making as an important practice

00:18:50.280 --> 00:18:53.310
of memorialization in the 19th century,

00:18:53.310 --> 00:18:54.687
and so on and so on.

00:18:54.687 --> 00:18:56.760
And of course, you know,
there's a clear sense

00:18:56.760 --> 00:19:01.760
in which those factors all
lining up in the right way,

00:19:02.190 --> 00:19:04.530
there was enormously unlikely, right?

00:19:04.530 --> 00:19:09.210
And that's why just witnessing
again that event here

00:19:09.210 --> 00:19:11.793
would not have let you predict this.

00:19:12.810 --> 00:19:17.220
And I think there's a general story here

00:19:17.220 --> 00:19:21.720
as to why it's so hard to
predict events in the future,

00:19:21.720 --> 00:19:26.100
especially the distant
future, from their causes.

00:19:26.100 --> 00:19:28.050
If you observe an event C here

00:19:28.050 --> 00:19:30.363
that's later going to produce an event E,

00:19:31.350 --> 00:19:32.850
whether E is going to happen,

00:19:32.850 --> 00:19:37.203
that's also going to depend
on many other causal factors.

00:19:38.280 --> 00:19:42.753
And if the event is in the distant future,

00:19:44.250 --> 00:19:46.590
often you're going to
need an enormous amount

00:19:46.590 --> 00:19:47.850
of such factors,

00:19:47.850 --> 00:19:49.200
and the probability that they're going

00:19:49.200 --> 00:19:52.200
to line up in the right way,
it's going to be very low.

00:19:52.200 --> 00:19:55.320
And that's why observing the causal arm,

00:19:55.320 --> 00:19:59.100
that's not going to tell you
that the effect happened.

00:19:59.100 --> 00:20:02.013
The probability of that
is going to be very low.

00:20:05.612 --> 00:20:08.117
And so, one lesson here
is that I mentioned

00:20:09.360 --> 00:20:11.370
this epistemic predicament that we face,

00:20:11.370 --> 00:20:15.000
that we can only make local observations,

00:20:15.000 --> 00:20:18.630
but we live in a world
governed by global laws, right?

00:20:18.630 --> 00:20:21.630
Well, one idea here is that,

00:20:21.630 --> 00:20:25.203
at least when it comes
to predicting the future,

00:20:26.160 --> 00:20:29.370
that predicament is very severe, right?

00:20:29.370 --> 00:20:30.810
The fact that the laws are global

00:20:30.810 --> 00:20:32.778
means that a local cause

00:20:32.778 --> 00:20:35.310
is never going to be
sufficient for its effect.

00:20:35.310 --> 00:20:39.093
You're always going to need
more factors to entail it.

00:20:40.830 --> 00:20:42.900
And that means, you know, to
make a reliable predictions,

00:20:42.900 --> 00:20:45.870
you would have to be able
to observe all of this.

00:20:45.870 --> 00:20:46.980
But that's going to be spread

00:20:46.980 --> 00:20:49.650
over an enormous spatial region.

00:20:49.650 --> 00:20:50.790
And of course, you know,

00:20:50.790 --> 00:20:53.130
we can't make observations
like that, right?

00:20:53.130 --> 00:20:56.793
We're limited to observing
the things around us.

00:20:59.310 --> 00:21:01.830
Again, this is highly abstract story,

00:21:01.830 --> 00:21:02.880
but I hope it makes sense,

00:21:02.880 --> 00:21:05.940
and perhaps it strikes you
as perfectly trivial, even,

00:21:05.940 --> 00:21:06.773
hopefully.

00:21:07.770 --> 00:21:08.910
Where things get interesting

00:21:08.910 --> 00:21:11.670
is if you think about what
happens in the reverse direction,

00:21:11.670 --> 00:21:16.470
when you go from effect to cause.

00:21:16.470 --> 00:21:18.330
So now, when you're reading the plaque

00:21:18.330 --> 00:21:20.670
and learning about this event here,

00:21:20.670 --> 00:21:22.657
'cause I said, you know,

00:21:22.657 --> 00:21:23.490
here we wanna say intuitively

00:21:23.490 --> 00:21:26.130
that the probability of the
cause, given the effect here,

00:21:26.130 --> 00:21:28.740
it's very high, right?

00:21:28.740 --> 00:21:31.140
But it's sort of a mystery
how that can be the case,

00:21:31.140 --> 00:21:34.260
because the global character
of the physical laws

00:21:34.260 --> 00:21:38.490
that makes it impossible
to predict the future,

00:21:38.490 --> 00:21:41.280
it's also present towards the past.

00:21:41.280 --> 00:21:43.396
The global character of laws,

00:21:43.396 --> 00:21:45.993
it's a time symmetric feature of the laws.

00:21:48.510 --> 00:21:49.770
And what that means

00:21:49.770 --> 00:21:54.300
is that, just like a local
cause is never sufficient

00:21:54.300 --> 00:21:57.180
for a later effect, given the laws,

00:21:57.180 --> 00:22:01.350
likewise, a local event
is never sufficient

00:22:01.350 --> 00:22:04.500
for its past cause, given the laws.

00:22:04.500 --> 00:22:06.663
The laws are always going to tell you,

00:22:08.370 --> 00:22:09.930
whether the cause really happened

00:22:09.930 --> 00:22:12.630
depends not only on that local events,

00:22:12.630 --> 00:22:17.297
but on what happens in an
enormous spatial region.

00:22:20.220 --> 00:22:22.290
Again, to figure out what happens here

00:22:22.290 --> 00:22:23.744
on the basis of there,

00:22:23.744 --> 00:22:26.730
the laws need information about everything

00:22:26.730 --> 00:22:27.980
that happens here, right?

00:22:30.210 --> 00:22:32.850
To make this a bit more concrete,

00:22:32.850 --> 00:22:36.720
one way to see that, you know,

00:22:36.720 --> 00:22:38.610
the case of our little plaque here,

00:22:38.610 --> 00:22:41.430
it's not sufficient given
the laws for this to happen

00:22:41.430 --> 00:22:42.330
or to have happened,

00:22:42.330 --> 00:22:46.230
is to think about all the other effects

00:22:46.230 --> 00:22:48.260
of the departure of The Mayflower

00:22:48.260 --> 00:22:49.500
that are sort of scattered

00:22:49.500 --> 00:22:52.080
in the macro state of the world right now.

00:22:52.080 --> 00:22:55.230
So this includes things like, you know,

00:22:55.230 --> 00:23:00.230
statements in encyclopedias,
monuments to The Mayflower,

00:23:01.290 --> 00:23:03.540
memories in the brains of historians

00:23:03.540 --> 00:23:06.210
and school children and so on, right?

00:23:06.210 --> 00:23:10.120
And imagine for a second
that all those other records

00:23:10.980 --> 00:23:13.110
say nothing about The Mayflower,

00:23:13.110 --> 00:23:15.900
or say that it departed at some other date

00:23:15.900 --> 00:23:17.283
or something, right?

00:23:18.510 --> 00:23:21.330
Well, presumably then the laws would say

00:23:21.330 --> 00:23:24.150
the plaque is most likely
the result of a mistake

00:23:24.150 --> 00:23:26.070
or prank or something like that.

00:23:26.070 --> 00:23:26.903
Right?

00:23:26.903 --> 00:23:28.500
Now the laws would say

00:23:28.500 --> 00:23:31.740
this past cause never really happened.

00:23:31.740 --> 00:23:33.900
So that's one way to see that, you know,

00:23:33.900 --> 00:23:35.280
whether this thing really happens,

00:23:35.280 --> 00:23:36.540
as far as the laws are concerned,

00:23:36.540 --> 00:23:39.873
it depends on what happens
globally over here.

00:23:40.800 --> 00:23:42.690
But again, you know, just seeing this here

00:23:42.690 --> 00:23:44.520
is enough to make the inference.

00:23:44.520 --> 00:23:47.103
So, that's sort of a mystery again.

00:23:49.118 --> 00:23:51.840
And this is again a general feature.

00:23:51.840 --> 00:23:54.033
There's nothing specific about my example.

00:23:54.930 --> 00:23:57.750
Whenever you see a local effect E,

00:23:57.750 --> 00:24:01.083
and you're trying to predict
a past cause C of it,

00:24:02.190 --> 00:24:04.410
well, whether C really happened,

00:24:04.410 --> 00:24:06.180
as far as the physical laws are concerned,

00:24:06.180 --> 00:24:11.070
that's also going to depend
on all the other effects of C.

00:24:11.070 --> 00:24:15.000
And if all those other effects are absent,

00:24:15.000 --> 00:24:17.010
or if they say something different,

00:24:17.010 --> 00:24:19.020
then the laws are going to tell you

00:24:19.020 --> 00:24:22.053
C most likely didn't happen, right?

00:24:24.000 --> 00:24:25.230
So, the upshot here is that we have

00:24:25.230 --> 00:24:27.870
this sort of deep symmetry
at the physical levels

00:24:27.870 --> 00:24:30.000
between cause to effect inference,

00:24:30.000 --> 00:24:32.550
and effect to cause inference, right?

00:24:32.550 --> 00:24:34.860
And the question is, "What
breaks the symmetry?"

00:24:34.860 --> 00:24:37.200
What makes it so that here,

00:24:37.200 --> 00:24:39.960
the probability of the effect
given the cause is very low,

00:24:39.960 --> 00:24:43.950
but here, the probability of
the cause given the effect,

00:24:43.950 --> 00:24:46.593
it can be very high.

00:24:48.630 --> 00:24:50.010
But I think if you think about it

00:24:50.010 --> 00:24:51.480
in terms of causal modeling,

00:24:51.480 --> 00:24:54.180
and the asymmetry between
causes and effects

00:24:54.180 --> 00:24:56.460
that's encapsulated in the
causal mark of condition,

00:24:56.460 --> 00:24:59.523
then the answer becomes clear.

00:25:02.760 --> 00:25:04.260
In a causal mark of condition,

00:25:04.260 --> 00:25:05.190
it's going to tell you,

00:25:05.190 --> 00:25:08.703
looking first at the sort
of cause to effect case,

00:25:10.980 --> 00:25:14.040
those variables here, C
and D are the causes of E.

00:25:14.040 --> 00:25:16.683
They're exogenous variables, right?

00:25:18.810 --> 00:25:21.010
So the causal mark of condition tells you

00:25:22.230 --> 00:25:25.470
there's not going to be
any correlation between C

00:25:25.470 --> 00:25:28.683
and the other causes of the
effect you're trying to predict.

00:25:30.210 --> 00:25:32.940
And that's why the probability of E

00:25:32.940 --> 00:25:36.603
given that cause alone has to be very low.

00:25:37.710 --> 00:25:39.060
One way to say this is simple,

00:25:39.060 --> 00:25:42.480
that C was correlated with
all those other conditions,

00:25:42.480 --> 00:25:44.163
X1, X2, and so on.

00:25:45.120 --> 00:25:46.620
Then the simple occurrence of C

00:25:46.620 --> 00:25:49.320
would make those conditions very likely,

00:25:49.320 --> 00:25:50.850
and therefore the occurrence of E

00:25:50.850 --> 00:25:53.310
also very likely as well, right?

00:25:53.310 --> 00:25:55.170
So here, just observing C

00:25:55.170 --> 00:25:56.850
would provide a lot of information

00:25:56.850 --> 00:26:00.330
about what's going to
happen in the future.

00:26:00.330 --> 00:26:04.080
That's precisely the sort of correlation

00:26:04.080 --> 00:26:06.360
between exogenous
variables that's forbidden

00:26:06.360 --> 00:26:08.043
by the causal mark of condition.

00:26:08.910 --> 00:26:10.230
In the case of The Mayflower,

00:26:10.230 --> 00:26:12.600
the causal mark of condition says

00:26:12.600 --> 00:26:14.190
you're not going to find a correlation

00:26:14.190 --> 00:26:17.760
between, say, the departure
of a ship from a harbor,

00:26:17.760 --> 00:26:20.940
and all the other conditions required

00:26:20.940 --> 00:26:24.513
to make that departure
memorable four centuries later.

00:26:27.090 --> 00:26:30.095
And that's why just observing
the departure of the ship

00:26:30.095 --> 00:26:33.573
doesn't make the effect likely at all.

00:26:36.570 --> 00:26:41.550
And the reason that this
doesn't apply in reverse

00:26:41.550 --> 00:26:46.080
is that here, now, we
are dealing with effects

00:26:46.080 --> 00:26:47.790
of a common cause, right?

00:26:47.790 --> 00:26:49.650
The effect you're observing

00:26:49.650 --> 00:26:52.067
and all the other effects of C,

00:26:52.067 --> 00:26:53.670
they're the effect of a common cause.

00:26:53.670 --> 00:26:55.923
And here, the causal
mark of condition says,

00:26:56.833 --> 00:27:00.660
you know, those things can and
generally will be correlated

00:27:00.660 --> 00:27:01.510
with one another.

00:27:03.990 --> 00:27:08.280
And that means the probability
of C, even E alone,

00:27:08.280 --> 00:27:09.933
again can be very high.

00:27:11.160 --> 00:27:14.190
All that this requires is that
there be a strong correlation

00:27:14.190 --> 00:27:18.483
between E and the other effects of C.

00:27:19.470 --> 00:27:22.320
That's all that's required
for this thing over here

00:27:22.320 --> 00:27:24.183
to make that thing very likely.

00:27:25.470 --> 00:27:28.473
So if you think of it
in terms of my example,

00:27:29.490 --> 00:27:33.090
again, plaques generally
make the events they report

00:27:33.090 --> 00:27:34.200
very likely, right?

00:27:34.200 --> 00:27:36.543
Plaques are reliable in general.

00:27:38.023 --> 00:27:38.970
If you go around reading plaques,

00:27:38.970 --> 00:27:41.790
you're gonna get it
right most of the time.

00:27:41.790 --> 00:27:45.210
How can that be, given that
the events they report,

00:27:45.210 --> 00:27:46.830
as far as the laws are concerned,

00:27:46.830 --> 00:27:49.320
also depend on many other things

00:27:49.320 --> 00:27:51.750
in the macro state of the world?

00:27:51.750 --> 00:27:54.240
Well, the answer is that
the content of those plaques

00:27:54.240 --> 00:27:57.360
is strongly correlated with other records

00:27:57.360 --> 00:27:58.950
of those past events,

00:27:58.950 --> 00:28:02.160
of, you know, memories in
the brains of historians,

00:28:02.160 --> 00:28:06.560
monuments, what encyclopedias
say and so on, right?

00:28:06.560 --> 00:28:09.900
So that's why you don't need
to consult the encyclopedias

00:28:09.900 --> 00:28:11.820
or ask the historian and so on, right?

00:28:11.820 --> 00:28:13.520
Just reading the plaque is enough.

00:28:16.590 --> 00:28:19.290
So the upshot here is
that if that's right,

00:28:19.290 --> 00:28:20.820
it's really the causal mark of condition

00:28:20.820 --> 00:28:23.790
that explains why we can
infer so much about the past,

00:28:23.790 --> 00:28:26.820
but little about the future.

00:28:26.820 --> 00:28:28.650
And again, to repeat this one more time,

00:28:28.650 --> 00:28:31.470
the background here is
that we face this problem

00:28:31.470 --> 00:28:33.150
that the physical laws are global,

00:28:33.150 --> 00:28:35.850
but we can only make local observations.

00:28:35.850 --> 00:28:39.300
So there's this mystery as to
how we can ever infer anything

00:28:39.300 --> 00:28:42.450
from those local observations.

00:28:42.450 --> 00:28:44.554
And the thought is the distinction

00:28:44.554 --> 00:28:45.644
between causes and effects

00:28:45.644 --> 00:28:47.850
explains why that
predicament is really severe

00:28:47.850 --> 00:28:50.250
when we try to infer the future,

00:28:50.250 --> 00:28:54.123
but it's not so severe when
we try to infer the past.

00:28:55.800 --> 00:28:58.500
And the idea is, you know, if
you make a local observation,

00:28:58.500 --> 00:29:00.990
if you see a local event over here,

00:29:00.990 --> 00:29:05.160
it's not going to be correlated
with all the other causes

00:29:05.160 --> 00:29:07.710
of the effects it's going
to produce in the future.

00:29:07.710 --> 00:29:10.290
So the observation by
itself doesn't tell you much

00:29:10.290 --> 00:29:14.340
about the future, but the
local event you observe,

00:29:14.340 --> 00:29:15.570
it's going to be correlated

00:29:15.570 --> 00:29:19.053
with all the other effects
of its past causes.

00:29:19.950 --> 00:29:22.620
And that's why your
local observation alone

00:29:22.620 --> 00:29:27.183
can give you a lot of
information about the past.

